Nbd7 9. Ngf3 Ne4 10. Bh4 c5 11. O-O Ndf6 12. Ne5 Nxd2 13. Qxd2 Ne4 14. Qe2 Qc7
15. Rad1 Rad8 16. Kh1 f6 17. Ng4 Kh8 18. Nf2 f5 19. Nxe4 fxe4 20. Bb5 c4 21. g4
a6 22. Ba4 b5 23. Bc2 Qd7 24. Rf2 Bf6 25. Bg3 a5 26. f5 g5 27. h4 h6 28. Rh2
Kg7 29. Kg2 Rh8 30. Rdh1 Rdg8 31. Kf1 Qe8 32. Qf2 b4 33. Be5 b3 34. axb3 cxb3
35. Bd1 a4 36. hxg5 hxg5 37. Rh5 Rxh5 38. Rxh5 Rh8 39. Rxg5+ Kf8 40. Kg2 Bxg5
41. Bxh8 Kf7 42. Be5 a3 43. bxa3 Qb5 44. f6 $1 b2 $2 (44... exf6 45. Bxf6 $1
Bxf6 46. g5 Qb6 47. Bxb3 $18) (44... Bc8 $1) 45. fxe7+ Kxe7 46. Qf5 b1=Q 47.
Qxg5+ Ke8 48. Bd6 $2 {[#]Here Daniel went into a long think, wrote down 48...
Kf7, which gets mated after 49 Qf5+, crossed it out and wrote 48...Qf1+, which
draws by perpetual. Desperately trying to keep a straight poker face I waited
for the inevitable axe to all, only to be stunned as Daniel scribbled out this
move (not believing the checks would lead anywhere) and played his
"improvement"! (Tim Wall, bulletin)} (48. Qg8+ $1 Ke7 49. Qg7+ Ke6 50. Qf6+ Kd7
51. Qf7+ Kd8 52. Qc7+ Ke8 53. Bd6 Qd7 54. Ba4 Qc2+ 55. Bxc2 Qxg4+ 56. Bg3 $18)
48... Q5b2+ $4 49. Kg3 Kd7 50. Ba4+ {Source: bulletin} 1-0

